$DIR/main.p:23:14: cannot convert out.String() to an interface: it points into memory allocated from a, a local arena of builder, which is gone before the interface's copy is released
    out.String() points into memory allocated from a, a local arena of builder.
    a (a local arena of builder, declared at main.p:19) was given as an
    allocator to a call that returns a string, or to a value that returns one,
    and such text may have been allocated from the arena. What lives in the
    arena is gone when the arena is. Converting out.String() to an interface
    puts a copy of it in builder's frame, and the copy holds a reference to the
    text until builder returns. That is after the arena a is dropped, and
    releasing the reference would write into the arena's memory.
    Go keeps memory alive for as long as any pointer refers to it. Plutonium has
    no garbage collector: an owner releases its memory when it is dropped, so a
    view must not be used after the value it looks at has gone.
    Use the text without converting it to an interface, for example by writing
    its bytes:
        os.Stdout.Write(b.Bytes())
    Or convert a string made from an allocator that outlives the arena, passed
    explicitly, or from the default allocator before the arena is installed
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3313" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:32:14: cannot convert s to an interface: it points into memory allocated from a, a local arena of window, which is gone before the interface's copy is released
    s points into memory allocated from a, a local arena of window.
    alloc.Use(&a) (at main.p:30) makes a (a local arena of window) the default
    allocator, so what is allocated through the default allocator from there on
    (by new, a string operation, or a call that allocates and leaves out its
    allocator) lives in the arena, and is gone when the arena is. Converting s
    to an interface puts a copy of it in window's frame, and the copy holds a
    reference to the text until window returns. That is after the arena a is
    dropped, and releasing the reference would write into the arena's memory.
    Go keeps memory alive for as long as any pointer refers to it. Plutonium has
    no garbage collector: an owner releases its memory when it is dropped, so a
    view must not be used after the value it looks at has gone.
    Use the text without converting it to an interface, for example by writing
    its bytes:
        os.Stdout.Write(b.Bytes())
    Or convert a string made from an allocator that outlives the arena, passed
    explicitly, or from the default allocator before the arena is installed
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3313" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:42:9: cannot return out.String(), which points into memory allocated from a, a local arena of leak
    out.String() points into memory allocated from a, a local arena of leak. a
    (a local arena of leak, declared at main.p:38) was given as an allocator to
    a call that returns a string, or to a value that returns one, and such text
    may have been allocated from the arena. What lives in the arena is gone when
    the arena is. When leak returns, the arena a is gone, so out.String() would
    point at memory that is no longer allocated.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    Allocate what must outlive the arena from an allocator that does, passed
    explicitly:
        heap := alloc.Default() // before alloc.Use
        ...
        v := vec.New[T](heap)
    Or install an arena that outlives the result, such as one the caller passes
    in, or make the copy that must outlive the arena after the Restore is
    dropped.
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3001" for a longer explanation)
